# Test: ze doctor --json reports an L2TP config the host's kernel cannot carry
# VALIDATES: the l2tp component's two kernel capability enrolments (l2tp, the
# CONFIG_L2TP control family; l2tp-ppp, the CONFIG_PPPOL2TP session socket) are
# reachable from the USER entry point. A forced absent l2tp is an ERROR, so
# `ze doctor` exits 1, and the message names the kernel feature and the
# configuration that asked. A forced cannot-determine l2tp-ppp is a separate
# WARNING row with its own code, so both enrolments are proved in one run.
# PREVENTS: an L2TP LNS that accepts a configuration on a kernel with no L2TP
# dataplane and carries no session. This replaces doctor-l2tp-module.ci, whose
# /proc/modules check was deleted with ad0931ae26 (its ledger row is in that commit).
#
# The probes are FORCED so both branches are reached on a host whose kernel is
# healthy; without the override this would pass only where L2TP happens to be
# missing (ai/rules/interop-and-goal-validation.md). The enrolment is Linux-only
# (internal/component/l2tp/register_kernelcap_linux.go), so off Linux ze doctor
# emits no l2tp row at all.
option=needs-linux

option=env:var=ze.test.kernelcap.force:value=l2tp=absent,l2tp-ppp=unknown

tmpfs=l2tp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
l2tp {
    enabled true
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze doctor --json config l2tp.conf
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stdout:contains=doctor-l2tp-unavailable
expect=stdout:contains=CONFIG_L2TP
expect=stdout:contains=doctor-l2tp-ppp-unknown
expect=stdout:contains=CONFIG_PPPOL2TP
# The forced answer must reach the operator, so the injection is never mistaken
# for the host's own verdict.
expect=stdout:contains=ze.test.kernelcap.force
reject=stdout:contains=doctor-l2tp-ppp-unavailable
